{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01TCLDN8UCJBUy9a8tdxGLjK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The complete public ABI of the C shared library (per `nm -D`) is a single\n4\t//! exported symbol:\n5\t//!\n6\t//!   * `to_barycentric`\n7\t//!\n8\t//! The public header declares:\n9\t//!\n10\t//! ```c\n11\t//! typedef struct lm_vec2 { float x, y; } lm_vec2;\n12\t//! lm_vec2 to_barycentric(lm_vec2 p1, lm_vec2 p2, lm_vec2 p3, lm_vec2 p);\n13\t//! ```\n14\t//!\n15\t//! `lm_v2`, `lm_sub2` and `lm_dot2` are `static` in the C translation unit, so\n16\t//! they are deliberately *not* exported here either.\n17\t//!\n18\t//! # Bit-exactness\n19\t//!\n20\t//! Every value is computed in single precision (`f32`) in exactly the same\n21\t//! order as the C source, so ordinary results are bit-identical.\n22\t//!\n23\t//! For NaN operands the *sign and payload* of the result additionally depend on\n24\t//! which operand the x86 SSE instruction uses as its destination: `mulss`,\n25\t//! `addss`, `subss` and `divss` return the destination operand (quieted) when\n26\t//! it is NaN, and only otherwise propagate the source operand. When both\n27\t//! operands are NaN the destination therefore wins, and the two NaNs may have\n28\t//! different signs -- x86 manufactures the \"indefinite\" QNaN `0xFFC00000`\n29\t//! (negative) for invalid operations such as `0 * inf` or `inf - inf`, while a\n30\t//! propagated NaN usually keeps a positive sign.\n31\t//!\n32\t//! The reference C library is built by `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` with no\n33\t//! `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, i.e. unoptimized, and its codegen does *not* uniformly\n34\t//! use the left operand as the destination. From the reference disassembly of\n35\t//! `lm_dot2` (`a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y`):\n36\t//!\n37\t//! ```text\n38\t//! mulss  %xmm0,%xmm1     ; xmm1 = a.x * b.x   -> destination is a.x  (left)\n39\t//! mulss  %xmm2,%xmm0     ; xmm0 = b.y * a.y   -> destination is b.y  (RIGHT)\n40\t//! addss  %xmm1,%xmm0     ; xmm0 = (b.y*a.y) + (a.x*b.x) -> destination is the\n41\t//!                        ;                                 y term   (RIGHT)\n42\t//! ```\n43\t//!\n44\t//! A plain Rust `a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y` lets LLVM choose the commuted operand\n45\t//! order, which flips the sign of the resulting NaN for some inputs. To stay\n46\t//! byte-identical, the arithmetic below is expressed through primitives that\n47\t//! pin the destination operand via inline assembly, mirroring the reference\n48\t//! codegen instruction for instruction.\n49\t\n50\t#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]\n51\t\n52\t/// Mirrors `typedef struct lm_vec2 { float x, y; } lm_vec2;`\n53\t///\n54\t/// An 8-byte `repr(C)` struct of two `float`s, which the System V AMD64 ABI\n55\t/// classifies as SSE and therefore passes and returns in the low half of a\n56\t/// single XMM register -- matching the C library exactly.\n57\t#[repr(C)]\n58\t#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, Default, PartialEq)]\n59\tpub struct lm_vec2 {\n60\t    pub x: f32,\n61\t    pub y: f32,\n62\t}\n63\t\n64\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n65\t// Scalar primitives with an explicitly pinned destination operand.\n66\t//\n67\t// Each `*_ss(d, s)` computes `d <op> s` with `d` as the x86 destination\n68\t// operand, which fixes NaN selection exactly as the reference build does.\n69\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n70\t\n71\t#[cfg(any(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_arch = \"x86\"))]\n72\tmacro_rules! sse_op {\n73\t    ($name:ident, $mnemonic:literal) => {\n74\t        #[inline(always)]\n75\t        pub(super) fn $name(d: f32, s: f32) -> f32 {\n76\t            let mut d = d;\n77\t            // SAFETY: a single side-effect-free SSE arithmetic instruction on\n78\t            // two scalar float register operands. SSE is baseline on x86_64.\n79\t            unsafe {\n80\t                core::arch::asm!(\n81\t                    concat!($mnemonic, \" {d}, {s}\"),\n82\t                    d = inout(xmm_reg) d,\n83\t                    s = in(xmm_reg) s,\n84\t                    options(pure, nomem, nostack, preserves_flags),\n85\t                );\n86\t            }\n87\t            d\n88\t        }\n89\t    };\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\t#[cfg(any(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_arch = \"x86\"))]\n93\tmod ops {\n94\t    sse_op!(mul_ss, \"mulss\");\n95\t    sse_op!(add_ss, \"addss\");\n96\t    sse_op!(sub_ss, \"subss\");\n97\t    sse_op!(div_ss, \"divss\");\n98\t}\n99\t\n100\t/// Portable fallback for non-x86 targets: numerically identical, but NaN\n101\t/// sign/payload selection is left to the target's own FP semantics.\n102\t#[cfg(not(any(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_arch = \"x86\")))]\n103\tmod ops {\n104\t    #[inline(always)]\n105\t    pub(super) fn mul_ss(d: f32, s: f32) -> f32 {\n106\t        d * s\n107\t    }\n108\t    #[inline(always)]\n109\t    pub(super) fn add_ss(d: f32, s: f32) -> f32 {\n110\t        d + s\n111\t    }\n112\t    #[inline(always)]\n113\t    pub(super) fn sub_ss(d: f32, s: f32) -> f32 {\n114\t        d - s\n115\t    }\n116\t    #[inline(always)]\n117\t    pub(super) fn div_ss(d: f32, s: f32) -> f32 {\n118\t        d / s\n119\t    }\n120\t}\n121\t\n122\tuse ops::{add_ss, div_ss, mul_ss, sub_ss};\n123\t\n124\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n125\t// Translations of the C translation unit's `static` helpers.\n126\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n127\t\n128\t/// `static lm_vec2 lm_v2(float x, float y)`\n129\t#[inline(always)]\n130\tfn lm_v2(x: f32, y: f32) -> lm_vec2 {\n131\t    lm_vec2 { x, y }\n132\t}\n133\t\n134\t/// `static lm_vec2 lm_sub2(lm_vec2 a, lm_vec2 b)` -- both subtractions use the\n135\t/// left operand as the destination, as in the reference codegen.\n136\t#[inline(always)]\n137\tfn lm_sub2(a: lm_vec2, b: lm_vec2) -> l